= Reasoning =

== Overview ==
'''Reasoning''' is the use of information, representations, or premises to form conclusions, judgments, explanations, plans, or decisions.

Reasoning can be deliberate or relatively automatic, formal or informal, certain or uncertain, and individual or socially distributed.

== Reasoning and inference ==
[[Inference]] is the broader relation or process of deriving or supporting conclusions.

Psychological reasoning research studies how people actually generate and evaluate conclusions.

Formal logic studies whether conclusions follow under specified systems.

Therefore:

<pre>
human reasoning ≠ formal entailment
</pre>

People can reason well without explicit formal proof, and can also make systematic errors.

== Major forms ==
Important forms include:

* deductive reasoning;
* inductive reasoning;
* abductive reasoning;
* analogical reasoning;
* causal reasoning;
* probabilistic reasoning;
* practical reasoning.

Wave 5 treats deduction, induction, and abduction as separate formal/general concepts.

== Knowledge and representation ==
Reasoning depends on how a problem is represented.

Background [[Knowledge]], language, mental models, diagrams, examples, and external tools can change which inferences are easy or difficult.

== Heuristics and biases ==
People often use simplified strategies.

Heuristics can reduce computational effort and work well in appropriate environments, but can also produce predictable departures from a selected normative standard.

[[Cognitive bias]] research studies some of these patterned departures.

== Dual-process accounts ==
Some theories distinguish fast/intuitive and slower/deliberative processing.

This family of models is influential but should not be treated as literally two anatomical systems or as a complete explanation of every reasoning effect.

